# Local qualification evidence and per-checkout runtime scratch state. The
# managed user-level runtime home is ~/.youtab-agent-runtime, outside the repo.
.youtab/

# Desktop/bootstrap install marker written into the managed checkout root by the
# bootstrap installer. It is Youtab-managed runtime state, never a code change —
# ignore it so `youtab update`'s `git stash push --include-untracked` does not
# treat it as a local edit and autostash it on every run (#38529).
.youtab-agent-runtime-bootstrap-complete

# Persistent dev sandbox dir (scripts/dev-sandbox.sh --persistent)
.youtab/agent-runtime-sandbox/

# Interrupted-update breadcrumb + recovery lock written next to the shared venv
# by `youtab update` / launch-time self-heal. Runtime state, never a code change
# — ignore so `git status` stays clean and update's autostash skips them.
.update-incomplete
.update-incomplete.lock

# Checkout fingerprint the __pycache__ tree was last validated against
# (launch-time stale-bytecode sweep). Runtime state, never a code change.
.bytecode-fingerprint
.bytecode-fingerprint.tmp

# Installer-written method stamp in the managed checkout root (scripts/install.sh).
# Runtime metadata only — never a code change. Ignore so `git status` stays clean
# and `youtab update`'s untracked autostash does not treat it as a local edit (#66189 / #54855).
/.install_method

# Tool Search live-test harness output — non-deterministic model transcripts,
# regenerated by scripts/tool_search_livetest.py. Never an artifact of the repo.
scripts/out/

# Per-release changelog drafts. These exist only transiently during a release
# cut (passed to `gh release create --notes-file`); the GitHub Release itself
# stores the published notes. They are not a build artifact and must never be
# committed to the repo root. See the youtab-release skill.
RELEASE_v*.md

# Desktop demo-run scratch output (youtab writes demo/*.txt during recorded
# walkthroughs). Throwaway artifacts, never part of the app.
apps/desktop/demo/

# PR infographics are rendered locally and embedded in PR descriptions via the
# image-provider (fal.media) URL — they are NEVER committed to the repo. The
# PR body is the archive. See the youtab-agent-runtime-dev skill's
# pr-infographic-workflow reference (storage rule + lapse #8 / #COMMIT-1).
#
# Spelling variants are listed because a single `infographic/` pattern was
# sidestepped by an `infograficos/` directory (#70552). .gitignore is only
# the first line of defence and cannot stop `git add -f` at all — the
# infographic-check CI job is what actually enforces this.
infographic/
infographics/
infograficos/
infografico/
native/fts5_cjk/*.so
# Runtime marker written by youtab update when a lazy dependency refresh is
# interrupted; consumed by launch-time recovery. Never commit it (was tracked
# by accident via 3a69e34702, removed in the #72002 salvage).
.lazy-refresh-incomplete

# Historical intake and review artifacts are kept outside product history.
/.plans/
/.github/pr-screenshots/
/apps/desktop/pr-assets/
/sqlite_leak_fix.png

# Windows pytest litter: tmp_path (C:\Users\...\AppData\Local\Temp\pytest-*) interpolated
# into a bash command lands in the repo root with the drive colon mangled to U+F03A.
*AppDataLocalTemppytest-*

# ── Consented human speech, and everything derived from it ───────────────────
# Qualifying the wake word needs recordings of real people. Those recordings
# were given for one purpose, on the promise that they stay on local disk: they
# are never committed, never uploaded, and never leave the machine that captured
# them. Neither are the feature tensors cut out of them or the transcripts
# written from them — a mel/embedding tensor is the speech, resampled, and a
# transcript is what the speaker said.
#
# Datasets belong OUTSIDE the checkout entirely (see
# scripts/wakeword/SPEAKER_RECORDING_PACKAGE.md). These patterns are the second
# line of defence for the case where one lands inside it anyway.
#
# This stops the accident — `git add -A` after a recording session. It cannot
# stop `git add -f`, exactly as the infographic rule above records. What
# actually enforces the rule is
# tests/tools/test_wakeword_no_human_data_committed.py, which fails on any
# TRACKED file that looks like human audio, a derived tensor or a transcript,
# and on any tracked text that names a speaker id or a capture root.
#
# No `!` exceptions. The artifacts this repository legitimately ships (the 33
# synthetic fixtures, samples.npz, jo.wav) are already tracked, and ignore rules
# do not apply to tracked files — so nothing here has to be re-added in normal
# work. Regenerating the fixture set is the one case that needs `git add -f`,
# and that is a deliberate act which should be reviewed rather than swept in by
# a wildcard: a `!.../audio/*.wav` exception would re-admit any stray recording
# dropped into that directory.
*.wav
*.m4a
*.m4b
*.mp3
*.aac
*.amr
*.flac
*.ogg
*.opus
*.aiff
*.aif
*.caf
*.wma
*.3gp
# Derived feature tensors. openWakeWord features are a lossy but perfectly
# recognisable encoding of the audio they came from.
*.npy
*.npz
# Speaker directories, whatever they are nested under. E001, E002, E003…
speaker_E*/
**/speaker_E*
# Transcripts and per-take logs of what a speaker said.
*transcript*.json
*transcripts*.json
*.transcript
# Filled consent records. The blank template
# (scripts/wakeword/CONSENT_RECORD_TEMPLATE.md) is tracked and does not match
# this; a filled one carries the speaker's name and contact details, which is
# the only personal data in the whole workflow.
consent-record-*
# Capture roots, if one is ever created inside the checkout by mistake.
wakeword-human/
wakeword-build/
recordings/

# Pinned Microsoft standalone prerequisite fetched and verified on the build machine.
/apps/bootstrap-installer/src-tauri/assets/MicrosoftEdgeWebView2RuntimeInstallerX64.exe
